  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry, seasonable weather for Halloween weekend \ncontinues into early next week. Clouds will increase on Sunday, but \nno rain is expected. \n\nMostly sunny skies and seasonable temperatures prevail across the \nlocal area this afternoon. A large area of high pressure has been \nbuilding southward into the Mid-Atlantic behind last night's (dry) \ncold front. Aloft, a large area of mid/upper-level clouds are \nstreaming eastward ahead of a sprawling, diffuse shortwave trough. \nThese clouds gradually overspread the area later today and persist \nthrough most of the weekend. However, there certainly will be some \nsunshine on Sunday. The weather also looks ideal for any evening \nfestivities this Halloween with trick-or-treating temperatures in \nthe mid 50s to lower 60s, light winds, and dry conditions. Chilly \ntonight with lows in the upper 30s to lower 40s, except upper 40s to \nnear 50 F at the immediate coast. Patchy frost is possible by \ntomorrow morning, but is not expected to be widespread enough to \nwarrant any advisories (plus it'll be early November so the\nconcern for minor impacts is lower).\n\nTemperatures warm a few degrees on Sunday and Monday, with highs \nranging from mid 60s to lower 70s. The warmest temps will tend to \noccur away from the coast due to continuing onshore flow keeping \ncoastal locations a few degrees cooler. Temps warm further Tuesday \nas a NNW-SSE ridge axis moves overhead. Highs should be in the 70s \nfor all of the area and interior portions of southern VA and NE NC \ncould approach the upper 70s. While some rain chances existed for \nthe Monday-Tuesday timeframe a few days ago, there is strong \nconsensus among the global ensembles, AI guidance, and NBM on\nvery low (<5%) PoPs, mainly due to unfavorable forcing aloft \nand dry low levels. Warm again on Wednesday with high temps \nranging through the 70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A chance for showers returns Thursday and Friday, \nbut confidence remains low. Above average temperatures are also \nexpected to continue through at least the first few days of November.\n\nThe seasonably warm temperatures continue into mid-late week as\nthat aforementioned ridge shifts offshore. A complex surface \nand upper-air pattern develops mid week, particularly over the \ncentral CONUS, with some potential for a few showers by late \nweek as these features approach our area. In terms of the\nspecifics, a strong low pressure system will initially move \nfrom the Rockies into the northern Plains and Midwest early in \nthe week, while a trailing cold front eventually tries to \nadvance toward the area in the Thursday-Friday timeframe. As \nmentioned by my previous forecaster, there remains lots of \ndifferent solutions with respect to the track, strength, and \ntiming of this system. The probability of widespread rainfall \n(and beneficial amounts) seems to generally be decreasing and \nmost of the ensembles show a relative minima in rainfall over \nour area. However, this still could change and we continue to \nadvertise a general 40-50% PoP from Thursday morning into \nFriday. Above average temperatures likely continue through the \nend of next week, with no potential freezes in the forecast as \nthe more substantial cold air remains in the northern half of \nCanada for the foreseeable future.",
